  <p><strong>Effective 24 August 2026.</strong></p>
  <h2>What Chef stores</h2>
  <p>On a local Codex host, household members, explicit dietary and safety requirements, preferences, planning defaults, weekly plans, grocery lists, saved recipes and meal outcomes are stored as readable Markdown under <code>~/Documents/Chef</code> by default. Advanced users and automated tests can use <code>CHEF_DATA_DIR</code> to select a different directory.</p>
  <p><code>MEMORY.md</code> stays compact. Detailed plans and shopping lists live in weekly files, and reusable recipe files exist only when the user explicitly asks to save, favourite or repeat a recipe.</p>

  <h2>When local files are unavailable</h2>
  <p>Some hosts cannot read or write local files. Chef can still plan and cook with the context in that conversation, but it must not claim that a preference, plan or recipe was saved. The host may separately provide its own memory features under its own terms.</p>

  <h2>What Chef does not store</h2>
  <p>Chef must not persist retailer credentials, cookies, payment information, screenshots or raw retailer pages. The plugin does not send local Markdown files to Daniel Ferguson.</p>
  <p>A host agent or optional Computer Use provider may process information under its own privacy terms. Review those terms before enabling browser automation.</p>

  <h2>Local protection and deletion</h2>
  <p>Chef's Markdown is protected only by your operating-system permissions, device security and backups. Chef does not provide application-level encryption, so anyone with access to the operating-system account or backups may be able to read it.</p>
  <p>You can inspect, edit, copy, back up or delete the folder yourself. Chef does not automate destructive deletion. Version 0.2 also never migrates, reads, modifies or deletes an existing <code>chef.sqlite3</code>; archived 0.1 data remains under your control.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Privacy questions can be sent to <a href="mailto:gday@danferg.com">gday@danferg.com</a>.</p>
